# Keep the build context lean AND stable. Both the run-container builds
# (containers/*/Dockerfile — the shared tooling builder in containers/tools/, which
# compiles every asset binary from crates/, plus the adversarial and performance
# images, which compile the Foray/Lattice tooling in their own stages) and the
# service-image builds (deployments/images/services.Dockerfile, which compiles the
# backend/auth/dispatcher/driver/artifacts/arena/publisher crates) use the
# repository root as their context. Almost every one of those builds is just a `cargo
# build` over the Rust workspace — nothing else — so this is an ALLOWLIST: ignore
# everything, then re-include only the paths cargo actually reads.
#
# ONE BUILD FROM THIS CONTEXT IS NOT A CARGO BUILD: containers/gg-ci/Dockerfile, which
# bakes gg's eleven program-language toolchains into an image CI hydrates from. It
# compiles nothing of this repository; what it reads is a slice of it (scripts/ci,
# packages, rust-toolchain.toml) so that the pinned installers can find the pins they
# install from. Those three are re-included below anyway, for the driver image's gg
# stage, which is why this image needed no widening — but it is a second consumer of
# them, and the notes below say so, because a reader pruning an entry whose consumer
# they cannot find is the mistake this file's own history records twice.
#
# And one build from this context answers to a different allowlist ON SOME HOSTS AND
# TO THIS ONE ON OTHERS: .devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile, which carries its own
# ubuntu.dockerfile.dockerignore beside it. BuildKit prefers a per-dockerfile ignore
# file over the context root's, so on a Docker host that file is what narrows the
# devcontainer's context and nothing here is consulted for it. That preference is not
# something the build can rely on being honoured — a rebuild driven by podman-compose
# resolved THIS file instead and died on the devcontainer's first COPY — so the five
# `!/.devcontainer/…` entries near the bottom make this allowlist sufficient on its
# own. The block above them carries the whole argument, including what it costs the
# seven `COPY . .` builds to admit them.
#
# (The per-asset-kind images — sprite, voxel, mc, … — no longer read the context at
# all: they only `COPY --from` the tooling builder. They are still built with the
# repo root as their context for consistency, and it costs them nothing because they
# read none of it.)
#
# WHAT THIS ALLOWLIST ACTUALLY WEIGHS, because this paragraph used to say "~100kB" and
# was wrong by three orders of magnitude for years: ~35 MB across ~2,300 files, nearly
# all of it the Rust workspace's sources (crates/ is ~22 MB, the eleven guest packages
# ~13 MB) — real build inputs, every one of them compiled or reflected by some image.
# The figure is worth keeping honest because it is the unit of the `COPY . .` snapshot
# each of the seven whole-context builds materialises on a cache miss. It was ~113 MB
# until the re-exclusions at the foot of this file went in; 78 MB of the difference was
# a directory git had never seen (see the `crates/desktop/binaries/` entry).
#
# Flipping to an allowlist (rather than enumerating things to exclude) means a
# change to anything cargo does NOT compile — the frontend, the docs, test cases,
# k8s manifests, models — can never invalidate a build layer. Only a change to a
# file in the list below busts the `COPY . .` cache, which is the whole point: those
# edits genuinely shouldn't trigger an image rebuild. The devcontainer's install
# scripts are the one exception the list makes to that, knowingly and for a reason
# the entry states.

# Ignore everything by default…
*

# …then re-include the Rust workspace and the few files a build embeds. All
# workspace members live under crates/, and no manifest reaches outside it, so
# this is the complete set cargo needs to resolve and compile any crate.
!/Cargo.toml
!/Cargo.lock
!/rust-toolchain.toml
!/.cargo
!/crates
# crates/core bakes these three trees into the binary verbatim via include_str!
# (the built-in orchestrator runner scripts, the harness manifests, and the
# built-in engine manifests), so they must be present in the context even though
# they are not Rust sources. A tree that is baked in but not re-included here does
# not fail at COPY like a missing Dockerfile source does — it fails much later, as
# `error: couldn't read ...: No such file or directory` from the compiler, which is
# how engines/ was missed when engines landed.
!/orchestrators
!/harnesses
!/engines

# The backend service image (services.Dockerfile, `--target backend`) bakes the
# Playwright browser-driver into its runtime to render references at ingest, so the
# driver script, the sibling ES modules it imports (its reporter-side `ttc` kit and
# the `validation` runtime), and its manifest must survive the allowlist too. Any
# file driver.mjs imports by relative path must be re-included here AND copied by
# the image Dockerfiles, or ingest dies with a runtime ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND. (These
# are the one non-cargo exception above; editing any of them does bust the backend
# image's COPY cache, but they change very rarely.)
!/packages/browser-driver/package.json
!/packages/browser-driver/driver.mjs
!/packages/browser-driver/validation.mjs
!/packages/browser-driver/ttc.mjs
!/packages/browser-driver/validation.mjs

# The adversarial and performance run-container images assemble a source-only
# "buildkit" — the verbatim engine crates plus a de-workspaced root Cargo.toml that
# re-supplies their `workspace = true` inheritance — which they `cp` from the build
# context and smoke-build standalone (see containers/{adversarial,performance}/Dockerfile).
# The crates ride in under !/crates, but these de-workspaced root manifests live
# outside it, so re-include them explicitly or the COPY/cp stages can't see them.
!/containers/adversarial/buildkit/Cargo.toml
!/containers/performance/buildkit/Cargo.toml

# The Blender run-container image (containers/blender/Dockerfile) COPYs its two
# authoring helpers — the importable glTF export module and the `tcab-blend`
# runner — straight from its own directory into the image (there is no cargo
# source to ride under !/crates for these). They live outside every path
# re-included above, so without this the COPYs fail with "not found" against the
# allowlisted context (transferring context: 2B).
!/containers/blender/tcab_blend_export.py
!/containers/blender/tcab-blend

# The gg language-toolchain builder (containers/gg-toolchains/Dockerfile) installs the
# Java arm's JDK and TeaVM jars by running the SAME script a developer's or CI machine
# runs, so there is one pinned list of versions rather than two: it COPYs
# scripts/ci/install-java.sh and the version file that script sources. Both live outside
# every path re-included above (nothing under crates/ reaches them), so without this the
# COPYs fail with "not found" against the allowlisted context (transferring context: 2B) —
# the same trap the Blender entry above records. That failure is not confined to Java:
# containers/build.sh is `set -euo pipefail` and builds this builder before every `-gg`
# variant, so a gg run image of ANY language stops being buildable.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-java/java-version.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-java.sh
# The Kotlin arm does the same, and its installer RUNS the Java one — it compiles Kotlin to
# JVM bytecode and hands that bytecode to the same TeaVM — so all four files are needed for
# either block to build.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-kotlin/kotlin-version.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-kotlin.sh
# The Swift arm does the same, and its installer does more than fetch: it PRUNES a 3.3 GB
# toolchain to the ~1 GB a wasm cross-compile actually runs, and vendors the shared libraries
# the published `lld` links against so the tree works on both the Debian-derived run images and
# `blender-gg`'s Ubuntu. That knowledge must live in one place, so the image runs the same
# script a developer's machine does.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-swift/swift-version.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-swift.sh
# The C++ arm does the same. Its installer prunes a ~650 MB wasi-sdk to the ~200 MB a wasm
# cross-compile actually runs and vendors the two GCC-runtime sonames the published binaries
# link, so the tree works on both the Debian-derived run images and `blender-gg`'s Ubuntu.
# That knowledge must live in one place, so the image runs the same script a developer's
# machine does.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-cpp/cpp-version.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-wasi-sdk.sh
# The C# arm does the same, and its version file SOURCES the C++ arm's (the two share a
# wasi-sdk pin and a wit-bindgen pin, deliberately, so that two arms reading one crates/gg/wit
# cannot describe the wire differently) — so its block needs both. Its installer prunes a
# ~770 MB .NET SDK to the ~122 MB a Roslyn compile actually runs, and deletes Roslyn's resident
# compiler server, which is a decision that must live in one place rather than two.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-csharp/csharp-version.sh
!/scripts/ci/install-dotnet.sh
# The PureScript arm does the same, and it is the arm where the version file and the image
# were LAST to stop disagreeing: its block used to restate `purs` and `esbuild` as ARG
# defaults that containers/build.sh overrode, so the Dockerfile carried a second, silently
# stale answer for anyone who built it without the wrapper. It runs the installer now. The
# third file is spago.yaml, which the version file is not merely beside but READS — the
# registry package set is written down where Spago reads it, and purescript-version.sh
# projects it — so sourcing that file without this line fails inside the image.
#
# The fourth is spago.lock, and it belongs to a different consumer: `install-gg-build-tools.sh`
# warms Spago's package STORE by running `spago install` in this package, and the lockfile is
# what decides which versions that resolves to. Without it the warm would resolve the package
# set afresh and cache a set the arm's build then re-resolves — a warm that warms the wrong
# thing is worse than none, because it looks like it worked.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/purescript-version.sh
!/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/spago.yaml
!/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/spago.lock
!/scripts/ci/install-purescript.sh

# The performance image bakes the committed training scenarios from the
# Lattice case's version folder (see containers/performance/Dockerfile's
# `COPY test-cases/.../references/training`). It is the one test-case tree a build
# embeds, so it must survive the allowlist; the path tracks the Dockerfile's COPY.
!/test-cases/performance/hard/lattice/v1.0.0/references/training

# The web service image (deployments/images/web.Dockerfile) is the one NON-cargo
# service image: it `npm ci`s the npm workspace and `vite build`s the web console
# (apps/web) into static assets nginx serves. So its build context needs the npm
# workspace too — but only the slice the console build reads:
#   - the root npm manifests (package.json + the lockfile npm ci validates against),
#   - EVERY workspace member's package.json (npm ci validates the whole workspace
#     against the lockfile, so a missing member's manifest aborts the install), and
#   - the SOURCE of the packages the console actually bundles: apps/web itself
#     plus its workspace deps @test-cabinet/ui and @test-cabinet/run-record, which
#     Vite resolves to their TypeScript sources (packages/ui exports ./src/*), and
#     @test-cabinet/run-stats, @test-cabinet/voxel-runtime and
#     @test-cabinet/particle-runtime, whose compiled subpaths the ui library
#     imports (run-stats' scoring rules and rollup; the two runtimes' `./three`).
#     The Dockerfile builds their dist/ before the console — the sources ride
#     along here, dist/ is re-excluded below and rebuilt in-image.
# node_modules/ and dist/ are re-excluded below (those re-exclusions come last and
# win), so a local install/build never rides along.
!/package.json
!/package-lock.json
!/tsconfig.base.json
!/apps/desktop/package.json
!/apps/docs/package.json
!/apps/site/package.json
!/apps/web
!/packages/run-record
!/packages/run-stats
!/packages/ui
!/packages/voxel-runtime
!/packages/particle-runtime
# The engine runtimes are the odd ones out in this block: the console does not
# bundle them and the ui library does not import them. They ride along for the
# BASE image below, whose staging script bakes them into the package store beside
# the shippable packages — an engine runtime is seeded per run rather than named
# by a case, so each appears in that script's list and in no case manifest.
!/packages/simple-2d
!/packages/structured-2d
!/packages/simple-3d
!/packages/structured-3d
# The shared engineless (`none`) validator harness and canvas recorder. Nothing
# bundles it and no COPY names it: scripts/stage-tcab-packages.mjs bakes it into the
# package store beside the engine runtimes, and crates/core's vitest validator
# (vitest_validator.rs, `stage_case_harness`) later copies its sources out of that
# store into a case's staged validator project. So the driver image must carry its
# sources, and a build context missing them fails inside the staging script
# ("shippable package @test-cabinet/case-harness not found under packages/"), taking
# every service image on services.Dockerfile down with it. scripts/ci/build-context.sh
# asserts every name in that script's SHIPPABLE list survives this allowlist, because
# a `COPY . .` stage is invisible to the COPY-source checks.
!/packages/case-harness
# The run-container BASE image (containers/base/Dockerfile) reuses this same npm
# slice: its builder stage `npm ci`s the workspace and runs the staging script to
# bake the shippable @test-cabinet packages — and the simple-2d engine runtime —
# into /opt/tcab-packages (see
# containers/README.md#the-shippable-test-cabinet-packages). The script is the one
# extra file that build needs beyond the console's slice, so re-include it.
!/scripts/stage-tcab-packages.mjs
# The driver service image (services.Dockerfile, `--target driver`) bakes in a
# static-musl `gg` harness, building it in a stage that runs
# scripts/build-gg-static.sh (which `cargo build`s test-cabinet-gg for the musl
# target). That build reaches much further into this repository than a `cargo build`
# normally does, and the next three blocks are why.
#
# COMPILING gg NOW MEANS REFLECTING ELEVEN SDKs — AND BUILDING EVERY ONE OF THEIR
# ARTIFACTS. gg's responses-as-code capability drives a model in one of eleven program
# languages, and TWO things per arm are generated on the way to a gg binary.
#
# What the model is *told* each language offers — every module, signature, argument, type
# and type member — is a signature catalogue reflected out of that language's own SDK by
# that language's own documentation tool. Those catalogues used to be committed under
# crates/gg/src/sandbox/guests/, which is what let this stage compile gg from crates/
# alone. They are not committed any more: crates/gg/build.rs runs
# scripts/gg-signatures.sh on every build, so the catalogue embedded in a gg binary is
# reflected from the SDK sources of the same checkout and cannot describe a surface the
# guest does not export.
#
# What a model's program is COMPILED AND EVALUATED AGAINST went the same way, one arm at
# a time, and the move is COMPLETE: an SDK jar, a compiler, a compiled library set, a
# guest archive, and finally the four baked interpreters — the TypeScript/JavaScript,
# Python, Ruby and C# guest components, 14 to 35 MB apiece. Every arm has a crate under
# crates/gg-sandbox-artifacts/ whose build script runs that arm's
# packages/gg-sandbox*/build.sh into its own OUT_DIR, and crates/gg include_bytes!s the
# result. So this stage does not merely read those packages' sources — it compiles them,
# and crates/gg/src/sandbox/guests/ does not exist any more.
#
# ONE ARM COSTS THIS STAGE SOMETHING THE OTHERS DO NOT, and it is not a context entry:
# csharp's build wants a whole .NET SDK and an unpruned wasi-sdk, ~1.4 GB installed by
# scripts/ci/install-gg-build-toolchains.sh — which is NOT part of the eleven-arm
# install-gg-toolchains.sh list every image already runs. A stage that builds gg needs
# both lists. That is a Dockerfile concern rather than a context one, and it is recorded
# here because this is where somebody reading "why does gg need all of packages/" will
# look for the rest of the price.
#
# The price of both is paid right here: the ten guest packages and the scripts that
# reflect and build them are build inputs of the driver image now, exactly as crates/ is.
#
# THE SECOND CONSUMER of everything in the next three blocks is containers/gg-ci, the
# image CI hydrates those toolchains from. It COPYs `packages` and `scripts/ci` as
# directories and needs each arm's `*-version.sh` for the pin and nothing else — so it
# would survive a narrowing that the driver stage above would not, and any narrowing has
# to be argued against the driver stage, which genuinely compiles the reflections. It is
# named here so that both consumers are findable from the entries rather than only one.
#
# So the whole of every guest package rides along. Each is a few hundred kB of sources,
# tools and pins (the big directories inside them — .build/, .spago/, output/ — are
# re-excluded at the end of this file), and each is named as a directory rather than as
# a file list on purpose: a reflection reads an arm's whole `src`/`Sources` tree, and
# the failure mode of missing one file is not a warning but "no such file" partway
# through an image build, minutes in.
!/packages/gg-sandbox
!/packages/gg-sandbox-python
!/packages/gg-sandbox-ruby
!/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript
!/packages/gg-sandbox-java
!/packages/gg-sandbox-kotlin
!/packages/gg-sandbox-rust
!/packages/gg-sandbox-swift
!/packages/gg-sandbox-cpp
!/packages/gg-sandbox-csharp
# The eleventh entry is not an arm, and that is exactly why it was missed: nothing is
# compiled or reflected out of `packages/gg-sandbox-jvm` on its own. It holds the
# canonical ABI, the wire encoding and the one vendored TeaVM runtime class that the java
# and kotlin arms BOTH compile into their own jars, so it is an input of two builds while
# looking like the package of none. Its absence took a `make local-rebuild` down
# mid-`cargo build` with `find: '/src/packages/gg-sandbox-jvm/src': No such file or
# directory`, reported by the kotlin arm's crate rather than by a COPY — which is why the
# COPY-reading half of scripts/ci/build-context.sh could not see it coming, and why that
# script now also asserts every `packages/gg-sandbox*` directory survives this file.
!/packages/gg-sandbox-jvm
# And the scripts that drive all of that, which is a CLOSURE rather than a file: the
# gg-build stage runs scripts/build-gg-static.sh, whose cargo build runs
# scripts/gg-signatures.sh, which runs the ten per-arm reflectors; the stage separately
# runs scripts/ci/install-gg-toolchains.sh, which runs eight per-arm installers and
# sources scripts/ci/lib.sh beside them. containers/gg-ci/Dockerfile runs that same
# installer from that same directory, so `!/scripts/ci` has two consumers, not one.
# The two entry points are named. The installer
# family is admitted as a DIRECTORY, because enumerating that half is precisely the trap
# this file's own history records twice — the Blender helpers and the Java arm's
# installer — and because it is the half that grows every time an arm is added. (The
# per-arm installer entries enumerated further up are now redundant with this, and stay:
# they exist for containers/gg-toolchains/Dockerfile, which COPYs each one BY NAME, and
# scripts/ci/build-context.sh reads this file path by path to check those named COPYs. A
# reader deleting one of them should have to think about that first.)
#
# What is deliberately NOT admitted is scripts/ as a whole, and the reason is this file's
# opening paragraph. Seven Dockerfiles built from this context do `COPY . .` — the shared
# asset-tooling builder that compiles ~20 Rust binaries, the adversarial and performance
# run images, the web console image and all three service targets — so anything admitted
# here busts the layer cache of every one of them. The ten guest packages above are worth
# that: the driver image genuinely compiles their reflections into the binary it bakes.
# The ~30 unrelated top-level scripts (media backfills, cluster re-ingests, sample-pack
# tooling, the desktop build helpers) are not, and admitting them would mean editing any
# one of them recompiled the asset tooling from scratch.
!/scripts/build-gg-static.sh
# The gg build's own shared shell, as a GLOB rather than as `gg-signatures.sh` alone. That one file
# was enough while reflecting the eleven catalogues was all the build did; it is not any more.
# `gg-signatures.sh` and `gg-artifacts.sh` both source `gg-arms.sh` (the one list of the arms), every
# arm's `build.sh` sources `gg-artifacts-out-dir.sh`, and the pinned-download and npm-tool resolvers
# are sourced by the arms' `bindings.sh` and by two installers. A glob rather than five lines for the
# reason the installer patterns above are globs: the set grows, and a list that has to be edited when
# it does is a list that will not be.
!/scripts/gg-*.sh
!/scripts/ci
# This image is also the one service Dockerfile that COPYs sibling files from its
# own deployments/images/ directory (the nginx server block, the config.js
# envsubst template, and the entrypoint that renders it) — the Rust images embed
# nothing from here. They live outside every path re-included above, so without
# this the COPYs fail with "not found" against the allowlisted context.
!/deployments/images/web

# The DEVCONTAINER image (.devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile). These five entries are
# the only ones in this file that exist for a BUILDER rather than for a Dockerfile,
# and the difference is worth a paragraph.
#
# That image builds from this same context — it bakes gg's eleven program-language
# toolchains, which are pinned by the repository rather than by .devcontainer/ — and
# it carries a per-dockerfile allowlist beside it,
# .devcontainer/ubuntu.dockerfile.dockerignore, narrowing that context to the ~230 kB
# slice its COPYs actually read. BuildKit prefers that file over this one, so on a
# Docker host it is what applies and nothing here is consulted for that build.
#
# THAT PREFERENCE IS NOT PORTABLE, and the failure it caused is quoted below. Buildah
# — so Podman, so podman-compose, so the devcontainer on a rootless-Podman host, which
# is a supported one (docker-compose.nixos.yml and .env.podman are committed and
# .devcontainer/README.md tells a NixOS user to copy them) — resolves an ignore file
# in this order (pkg/parse.ContainerIgnoreFile): an explicit --ignorefile, then
# <containerfile>.containerignore, then <containerfile>.dockerignore, then
# $CONTEXT/.containerignore, then $CONTEXT/.dockerignore. So it does have a sibling
# rule, and it still did not reach the devcontainer's file: a rebuild driven by VS
# Code's Dev Containers extension through podman-compose ended on the first COPY with
#
#     no items matching glob ".../.devcontainer/system/apt.sh" copied
#     (1 filtered out using /…/.dockerignore): no such file or directory
#
# — buildah naming THIS file, by absolute path, as the one it applied. Whether that is
# an older buildah than the sibling rule or the containerfile path podman-compose
# hands it, the conclusion does not change and is the reason these entries are here: a
# per-dockerfile ignore file is an optimisation the build may not assume was found, so
# the root allowlist has to be sufficient on its own. That is also how this was found —
# a rebuild that failed, not a prediction.
#
# The alternative fix — a root .containerignore, which Buildah prefers over
# .dockerignore — would repoint EVERY image built from this context on every Podman
# host at a second allowlist that has to be kept in step with this one. Five entries
# here cost less than that, and cost it once.
#
# WHAT THEY COST THE SEVEN `COPY . .` BUILDS, honestly: those layer cache keys gain
# ~24 small files, which is a real widening of the principle at the top of this file.
# In practice it is close to free. Those keys already carry the whole of crates/ (904
# commits in the last six months) and the whole of scripts/ci (68); .devcontainer's
# install scripts moved 24 times in the same window, and the image workflows carry no
# `paths:` filter (build-containers.yml says why), so a push touching only a
# devcontainer script triggers no image build to bust the cache of.
#
# What is deliberately NOT admitted is the rest of the directory — README.md,
# devcontainer.json, the four compose files, the Dockerfile and its ignore file. No
# COPY reads them, and they churn harder than the scripts do. The host-specific
# docker-compose.local.yml and .env are excluded for free by the same narrowing (the
# per-dockerfile allowlist, which admits !/.devcontainer whole, has to re-exclude
# them by name).
#
# These five entries and that per-dockerfile allowlist are ONE list with two
# spellings, and the wider one will not fail when they drift — a new script directory
# would work under Docker and break under Podman. scripts/ci/build-context.sh
# therefore checks every Dockerfile against BOTH allowlists that can apply to it.
!/.devcontainer/system
!/.devcontainer/languages
!/.devcontainer/tools
!/.devcontainer/ai
!/.devcontainer/post-install.sh

# Re-exclude build outputs and scratch that can appear UNDER the re-included
# paths, so a local `cargo build`'s target/ (or a stray node_modules) never bloats
# the context or churns the layer cache. These come last so they win.
**/target/
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/.astro/
# The guest packages' own scratch, which the block above would otherwise admit and
# which dwarfs everything else in this context: measured on a developer machine that
# has built every arm by hand, the ten packages are ~6 MB of sources and ~420 MB of
# these — 179 MB of PureScript `.build`, 123 MB of Rust, 75 MB of C#, 54 MB of Swift,
# 34 MB of `purs` output. Every one of them is regenerated inside the image by the
# reflection that needs it, so admitting them would cost a `make images` several
# hundred megabytes of context transfer to ship files the build immediately
# overwrites.
**/.build/
**/.spago/
**/__pycache__/
/packages/gg-sandbox-purescript/output/
# The Rust arm's WIT bindings are GENERATED into its source tree (bindings.sh writes
# src/bindings.rs, which is gitignored). They are a pure function of crates/gg/wit and
# the pinned wit-bindgen, and `signatures.sh` rewrites them UNCONDITIONALLY rather than
# when the file is missing — it spends a paragraph on why, and cites this line as the
# other half of the same invariant. So a copy carried in from a developer's machine
# would not be used; it would be overwritten. Keeping it out buys the two things that
# are left, which are enough on their own: a context that does not carry a file the
# build is about to rewrite, and a layer cache that does not churn on it.
/packages/gg-sandbox-rust/src/bindings.rs

# The desktop app's SIDECAR BINARIES, which are 78 MB of the ~113 MB this context
# used to weigh — two thirds of it, for a crate no image in this repository compiles.
# `crates/desktop/binaries/` holds the `k3d` and `kubectl` builds the Tauri app
# bundles; they are fetched at build time by scripts/fetch-desktop-sidecars.mjs (the
# release workflow runs it), never committed, and platform-specific — the pair on the
# machine this entry was written on are `*-aarch64-apple-darwin`, which no Linux image
# could execute even if one wanted to. The directory's own .gitignore is `*` plus
# `!.gitignore`, so git has never seen them and neither had this file: they arrived
# under `!/crates` like every other path below it.
#
# WHAT THEY COST is not the transfer, which BuildKit does incrementally, but the
# `COPY . .` snapshot every one of the seven whole-context builds materialises on a
# cache miss — 78 MB of it, unpacked into a layer, for `-p test-cabinet-artifacts`.
# A developer who has ever run the desktop app pays it on every service image build;
# one who has not pays nothing, which is exactly why this went unnoticed for so long.
/crates/desktop/binaries/
# And Tauri's generated schema tree, `crates/desktop/gen/` — gitignored output of the
# desktop crate's build script, regenerated whenever that crate is built. Small (~420 kB)
# and here for the churn rather than the size: it is a build output living inside a
# re-included source tree, which is the shape the block above exists to keep out.
/crates/desktop/gen/

# TypeScript's incremental build state. `packages/ui/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo` is gitignored
# output that lands inside `!/packages/ui` — re-included for the web console image, which
# reads that package's sources — and it is REWRITTEN by every local `tsc -b`. So a
# type-check on the host changes the context, which changes the `COPY . .` cache key, which
# recompiles the whole Rust workspace in an image that does not contain a line of
# TypeScript. As a glob rather than the one path, because the next package to gain a
# composite tsconfig should not have to rediscover this.
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# macOS Finder metadata, and the entry most likely to be the reason a `make images` you
# expected to be cached was not. `.DS_Store` files exist wherever a directory has been
# opened in Finder — this repository has them at its root and under crates/, packages/,
# scripts/, harnesses/, orchestrators/ and containers/, six of which sit inside allowlisted
# trees — and Finder rewrites them for reasons that have nothing to do with the repository:
# a window resized, a view mode changed, a folder scrolled. Each rewrite changes the
# `COPY . .` cache key of every build from this context, and the build stage's first act on
# a miss is to touch the tree and hand cargo a workspace it must compile from scratch. The
# symptom is a full ~19-crate rebuild with no source change, blamed on the Dockerfile.
#
# This is not hypothetical here: the primary development host is macOS running the tree
# through a Linux VM's shared mount, so Finder and the builder see the same directories.
**/.DS_Store
